{"key": "Medical::Medical-f79d978d", "time": "2026-10-09T15:25:20+00:00", "raw": {"candidates": [{"content": {"role": "model", "parts": [{"text": "When a lung nodule is found on imaging, the care team assesses the patient's risk for lung cancer, reviews the lung images, requests additional imaging scans if cancer is suspected, and performs a biopsy if needed to determine whether the nodule is cancerous. If the nodule is determined to be unlikely cancer, the team may refer the patient for lung cancer screening.", "thoughtSignature": ""}]}, "finishReason": "STOP", "groundingMetadata": {"groundingChunks": [{"retrievedContext": {"uri": "Medical.txt", "title": "Medical.txt"}}], "groundingSupports": [{"segment": {"endIndex": 258, "text": "When a lung nodule is found on imaging, the care team assesses the patient's risk for lung cancer, reviews the lung images, requests additional imaging scans if cancer is suspected, and performs a biopsy if needed to determine whether the nodule is cancerous"}, "groundingChunkIndices": [0]}, {"segment": {"startIndex": 260, "endIndex": 367, "text": "If the nodule is determined to be unlikely cancer, the team may refer the patient for lung cancer screening"}, "groundingChunkIndices": [0]}]}}], "usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 90, "candidatesTokenCount": 204, "totalTokenCount": 881, "trafficType": "ON_DEMAND", "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 90}], "candidatesTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 204}], "thoughtsTokenCount": 587}, "modelVersion": "gemini-3.8-flash", "createTime": "2026-10-09T15:24:10.065106Z", "responseId": "GgfJatL8A86w3dAPqoOouQc"}}
{"key": "Medical::Medical-d5964ccf", "time": "2026-10-09T15:29:41+00:00", "raw": {"candidates": [{"content": {"role": "model", "parts": [{"text": "Lung cancer begins in the cells lining the airways of the lungs, and recognizing risk factors, prevention strategies, and early detection options is vital for community health. Smoking tobacco is the leading risk factor, with cancer risk increasing the more and longer a person smokes, while secondhand smoke exposure also increases risk. Additional risk factors include exposure to carcinogens such as radon, asbestos, arsenic, beryllium, cadmium, chromium, nickel, diesel fumes, coal smoke, soot, silica, and air pollution, as well as being over 70 years of age, having prior radiation therapy to the chest, and genetic traits. For prevention, non-smokers should never start, and smokers are encouraged to use medical treatments and counseling to quit since new lung tumors are less likely to form after quitting. Eligible individuals may also consult their care teams about clinical trials exploring chemopreventive agents to prevent cancer. For symptom awareness, lung cancer can cause symptoms based on where a tumor grows or spreads, which can keep the lungs from getting the air they need, lead to paraneoplastic syndromes, or cause abnormal routine blood tests. Early detection through lung cancer screening aims to find cancer at an early stage before symptoms start, when treatment is most successful and can help prevent death. Because lung cancer screening is not recommended for everyone, individuals at risk should consult their healthcare provider to review their risk factors and determine if screening is appropriate for them.", "thoughtSignature": ""}]}, "finishReason": "STOP", "groundingMetadata": {"groundingChunks": [{"retrievedContext": {"uri": "Medical.txt", "title": "Medical.txt"}}, {"retrievedContext": {"uri": "Medical.txt", "title": "Medical.txt"}}, {"retrievedContext": {"uri": "Medical.txt", "title": "Medical.txt"}}, {"re